// The simulated world OpenArm v2 robots run in, as one MCP surface: the
// scene (assets, spawned objects, the robots and their placement), the
// controls its spawned objects offer (a desk's height), its light sources
// and the optical properties of its materials. Nothing here has a
// counterpart on the physical robot; the title says so, the instructions
// open by saying so, and every description says so, so a model reading
// this endpoint never mistakes it for a real-robot surface. Waldo alone
// implements object_controls, scene_lighting and scene_materials, so only
// a Waldo stack serves this document; the robots' own surface is the
// robot_control document, served on hardware and in simulation alike. What is
// spawned, and where it stands, is not read here: the engine's
// object_state contract reports it and stays inside the peppy framework,
// as the README's ground-truth rule says.
//
// This document is the whole artifact. A launcher lists it under
// `source: { exposures: ["simulation:v1"] }`, binds its four targets to
// the running simulation through `links`, and the server built into peppy
// serves it at `/simulation/v1/mcp`. The launchers hub serves it through
// the `world_control` axis of its `simulation_mcp` launcher, on port 8902, a
// process of its own beside the robots' endpoint `/robot_control/v1/mcp`
// on port 8900. It binds the simulation and nothing of any robot copy, so
// it outlives the copy.
{
  peppy_schema: "mcp_exposure/v1",
  manifest: { name: "simulation", tag: "v1" },

  // Advertised to clients through `server/discover`. `instructions` is the
  // prose a model reads before it decides what to call, and the only place
  // it learns the id scheme, the units, the bounds and the read-then-set
  // pattern: none of the contracts' comments reach it. The opening sentence
  // and the title suffix are the wording rule
  // test_no_simulation_ground_truth.py enforces on every document under
  // simulation/.
  server: {
    title: "Simulated world (simulation only)",
    instructions: "This endpoint configures a simulated world. It has no effect on and no counterpart in the physical robot. The tools edit the simulation the robots run in: scene.* for the scene (what the engine can place, the loaded scene, spawned objects, the robots standing and their placement), controls.* for what a spawned object lets you set (a standing desk's height, a conveyor's belt speed), lighting.* for its light sources and materials.* for the optical properties of its materials. Use it to set the world up and to check the setup, never to complete a task: to move an object, grasp it with a robot through the robots' endpoint; scene.move_object teleports and proves nothing about the robot. Read, then set: call scene.get_assets_list, scene.get_robots_list, controls.get_object_controls, lighting.get_lighting and materials.get_materials first, since every asset_id, robot, control id, light_id and material_id the other tools take comes from them, and every object_id is minted by scene.spawn_object. Positions are world-frame [x, y, z] in metres, Z up; headings are a yaw in radians about the world's +Z, counterclockwise seen from above, 0 facing +X; forces are world-frame newtons; masses are kilograms; durations are seconds; scale is uniform and 1 means as authored; directions are world-frame unit vectors pointing the way the light travels; colours are linear RGB in the unit interval; cone angles are half angles in radians; orientations are unit quaternions [x, y, z, w]. The simulated world's frame is not the frame of the robots' endpoint's poses: robot.move_arm takes poses in the robot's own frame, whose origin is the robot's base point on the floor, the point scene.get_robots_list reports as position, with +X the way the robot faces. For a robot standing upright, as every robot that joined the simulation does, a point p in that robot's own frame stands in the simulated world at position + Rz(yaw) * p, where Rz(yaw) turns p about +Z by the robot's listed yaw: [p.x * cos(yaw) - p.y * sin(yaw), p.x * sin(yaw) + p.y * cos(yaw), p.z]. A robot's name here is its name on the robots' endpoint: robot.list there names the same robots, and every tool there takes that name. Keep scale between 0.01 and 100, mass between 0 and 1000 kg and a push's duration_s between 0.01 and 30 s. A setter validates its whole request before it changes anything, a refused request changes nothing in the simulation and says why in message, and every setter answers with the effective value after the call, so read the response rather than assuming the request took. An object's controls are what its asset authors (only objects offering one are listed, an asset's description says when it does), each a scalar in the unit it reports within its min and max, standing at its default when spawned or at the value the loaded scene placed it at; a control in m or rad stands its part at the value within the call, like a teleport, whatever the object's physics, and anything resting on it is displaced, not carried, so set such a control before placing things on the part; a control in m/s runs its part's surface at that speed from the next physics step, a negative value the other way, and carries what rests on it. A lighting or materials change shows in every view the engine renders, the robot's cameras included, and touches no physics. Loading or clearing a scene removes every spawned object and restores that scene's authored lighting and materials, as lighting.reset_lighting and materials.reset_materials do on their own.",
  },

  targets: {
    // LOGICAL targets, not instances: each becomes a contract slot of the
    // server, and the launcher binds all four to the running simulation
    // engine. One target holds one contract, so the four contracts the
    // engine serves are four targets.
    scene: {
      contract: {
        name: "scene_manipulation",
        tag: "v1",
        // An author pin: the sha256 of the exact contract document this file
        // was written against (`sha256sum simulation/scene_manipulation.json5`
        // in the contracts hub). A launch that resolves other bytes for
        // `scene_manipulation:v1` refuses instead of publishing schemas this
        // file never saw.
        sha256: "dd5e40b73458f358b0f45993472c165b6b815dec0970953eb2e2fa70a828e211",
      },

      // The catalogue becomes a tool that answers within one request.
      services: [
        {
          member: "get_assets_list",
          tool: "scene.get_assets_list",
          description: "List the assets the simulation can place, as a JSON array in assets_json. Every entry carries at least asset_id, display_name, kind (scene or object) and category, and the engine may add fields of its own. The asset_id values, scene/<id> and object/<id>, are what scene.load_scene and scene.spawn_object take. Takes no arguments and changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
        {
          member: "get_robots_list",
          tool: "scene.get_robots_list",
          description: "List the robots standing in the simulation, one entry each in robots: robot, the name scene.move_robot takes and the name robot.list reports on the robots' endpoint, where every tool takes it; model, the robot catalogue id it stands as; position, its base's world-frame [x, y, z] in metres; yaw, which way the base faces, in radians about the world's +Z; attached, true for a robot that joined and false for one the world stands itself. Takes no arguments and changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
      ],

      // Each action becomes a tool. For a client that declares the MCP
      // tasks extension the call returns a task handle, `tasks/get` reports
      // its state, and `tasks/cancel` forwards to the action's cancel path;
      // for any other client the goal runs inside the call, which answers
      // with the terminal result. The contract declares no feedback topic,
      // so a goal reports its state transitions and the terminal result,
      // not interim progress.
      //
      // The bounds on scale, mass and duration_s live in the prose: an
      // action's goal fields take no `restrict` (the exposure format
      // narrows service requests only), so the engine's own refusal, with
      // its message, is what a goal outside them meets.
      actions: [
        {
          member: "load_scene",
          tool: "scene.load_scene",
          description: "Replace the simulated scene with the asset named by asset_id, which must be of kind scene (a scene/<id> from scene.get_assets_list); every spawned object is removed first and the scene's authored lighting and materials are restored. scale is a uniform scale applied to the scene, 1 = as authored; keep it between 0.01 and 100. success is false, with message saying why, when the scene is refused or fails to load.",
          operation: "long_running",
          // A scene load is the one goal that rebuilds the world, so it
          // gets the longer budget.
          deadline_ms: 60000,
        },
        {
          member: "clear_scene",
          tool: "scene.clear_scene",
          description: "Remove every spawned object from the simulation and restore its default scene, with that scene's authored lighting and materials. Takes no arguments.",
          operation: "long_running",
          deadline_ms: 60000,
        },
        {
          member: "spawn_object",
          tool: "scene.spawn_object",
          description: "Place one instance of an object asset in the simulation and report the object_id the engine minted for it. asset_id is an object/<id> from scene.get_assets_list; position is world-frame [x, y, z] in metres; yaw turns the object about the world's +Z in radians, 0 = as authored; scale is uniform, 1 = as authored, between 0.01 and 100; physics is dynamic (simulated, falls and collides), static (collides, never moves) or none (visual only); mass is the kilograms of a dynamic object, between 0 and 1000, and is ignored otherwise. object_id is empty on failure, when the asset is unknown, an argument is invalid or the spawn fails.",
          operation: "long_running",
          deadline_ms: 30000,
        },
        {
          member: "move_object",
          tool: "scene.move_object",
          description: "Teleport a spawned object in the simulation to a world-frame position [x, y, z] in metres, keeping its orientation and zeroing its velocity. object_id is the id scene.spawn_object reported.",
          operation: "long_running",
          deadline_ms: 30000,
        },
        {
          member: "remove_object",
          tool: "scene.remove_object",
          description: "Delete a spawned object from the simulation. object_id is the id scene.spawn_object reported.",
          operation: "long_running",
          deadline_ms: 30000,
        },
        {
          member: "apply_force",
          tool: "scene.apply_force",
          description: "Push a dynamic spawned object in the simulation with a constant world-frame force [x, y, z] in newtons for duration_s seconds, between 0.01 and 30. object_id is the id scene.spawn_object reported. The tool reports completion once the push is scheduled, not when it ends.",
          operation: "long_running",
          // Advertised to the client: this one sets something in motion.
          safety_sensitive: true,
          deadline_ms: 30000,
        },
        {
          member: "move_robot",
          tool: "scene.move_robot",
          description: "Relocate one simulated robot's base, named by robot as scene.get_robots_list reports it, to a world-frame position [x, y, z] in metres facing yaw, in radians about the world's +Z, keeping its joint configuration; every other robot stays where it stands. To move a robot without turning it, pass back the yaw scene.get_robots_list reports for it. The robot moves inside the simulation and nowhere else.",
          operation: "long_running",
          // Advertised to the client: this one moves the (simulated) robot.
          safety_sensitive: true,
          deadline_ms: 30000,
        },
      ],
    },

    controls: {
      contract: {
        name: "object_controls",
        tag: "v1",
        // Pinned like `scene` above (`sha256sum simulation/object_controls.json5`).
        sha256: "3cd46653b87f7f0ef0e243c317c12961febce9e449a44adbcc0a80c464ed24c1",
      },

      // The listing is a tool that answers within one request; the set is
      // an action, since a displacement rebuilds the world and a speed
      // waits for the next physics step, and its bounds are per control,
      // so they live in the listing and the engine's refusal rather than
      // in a `restrict`.
      services: [
        {
          member: "get_object_controls",
          tool: "controls.get_object_controls",
          description: "List every spawned object of the simulation that offers a control, in objects: object_id (the id scene.spawn_object reported), asset_id and controls, each with id (what controls.set_object_control takes), label, unit (m, m/s, rad), min and max (inclusive bounds), default (the value a spawned object starts at) and value (the current one). Bounds, default and value are world quantities: a length control on an object spawned at scale 2 reports twice its authored range. An object whose asset offers no control is not listed; an asset's description in scene.get_assets_list says when it offers one. Takes no arguments and changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
      ],
      actions: [
        {
          member: "set_object_control",
          tool: "controls.set_object_control",
          description: "Set one control of a spawned object in the simulation: object_id is the id scene.spawn_object reported, control an id from controls.get_object_controls, value a number in the control's unit within its min and max. A control in m or rad stands its part at the new value when the tool returns, like a teleport, whatever the object's physics, and anything resting on it is displaced, not carried, so set such a control before placing things on the part. A control in m/s runs its part's surface at the new speed from the next physics step, a negative value the other way, and carries what rests on it. A refused request (unknown object or control, a value not finite or out of bounds) changes nothing and says why in message. Answers with current_value, the value the control stands at after the call, 0 when the object or the control is unknown.",
          operation: "long_running",
          deadline_ms: 30000,
        },
      ],
    },

    lighting: {
      contract: {
        name: "scene_lighting",
        tag: "v1",
        // Pinned like `scene` above (`sha256sum simulation/scene_lighting.json5`).
        sha256: "9e66157ee1882c4f06f6cbbfadb02170603366c4562b671be6adc0849b10dd89",
      },

      // Each service becomes a tool that answers within one request. The
      // setters are `mutating`; `restrict` narrows the scalar request
      // fields at the endpoint, before the Peppy graph is touched, and the
      // engine's own per-light bounds apply inside them.
      services: [
        {
          member: "get_lighting",
          tool: "lighting.get_lighting",
          description: "Report every light of the loaded simulated scene as a JSON object in lighting_json: scene (the scene asset id), ambient_lux, fill_lux and lights, each with id, kind, label and properties keyed by property name (illuminance_lux, luminous_flux_lm, radiance_scale, color_rgb, position_m, direction, inner_angle_rad, outer_angle_rad, orientation_xyzw), each with unit, default, value and, for scalars, min and max. A light lists only the properties it supports. success is false, with message saying why, while no scene is loaded. Takes no arguments and changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
        {
          member: "set_light_intensity",
          tool: "lighting.set_light_intensity",
          description: "Set a simulated light's strength: illuminance in lux for a directional light, luminous flux in lumens for a point or spot lamp, or the radiance scale of an environment map (the map's stored radiance times this scale, in cd/m2). light_id comes from lighting.get_lighting; value must be finite, nonnegative and at most the light's reported max, and a refused request leaves the light as it was. Answers with current_value, the effective value after the call.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
          // The contract's `f64` is unbounded; the endpoint accepts a
          // nonnegative value up to ten million, the largest max any light
          // kind reports (10 Mlm for a lamp), and the light's own reported
          // max (200 klux for a directional light, 100 000 for a radiance
          // scale) applies inside that.
          restrict: { value: { min: 0, max: 10000000 } },
        },
        {
          member: "set_light_color",
          tool: "lighting.set_light_color",
          description: "Set a simulated light's colour, color = linear RGB [r, g, b] in the unit interval. light_id comes from lighting.get_lighting. Not supported by an environment light, whose colour is its map; a refused request leaves the light as it was. Answers with current_color, the effective colour after the call.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
        {
          member: "set_light_position",
          tool: "lighting.set_light_position",
          description: "Set where a simulated point or spot lamp sits, position = world-frame [x, y, z] in metres, Z up. light_id comes from lighting.get_lighting; a directional or environment light has no position and refuses, leaving the light as it was. Answers with current_position, the effective position after the call.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
        {
          member: "set_light_direction",
          tool: "lighting.set_light_direction",
          description: "Set which way a simulated directional light or spot lamp shines, direction = a world-frame vector [x, y, z] pointing the way the light travels; the engine normalises it and refuses a vector with no length. light_id comes from lighting.get_lighting; a point or environment light has no direction and refuses. Answers with current_direction, the effective unit vector after the call.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
        {
          member: "set_light_cone",
          tool: "lighting.set_light_cone",
          description: "Set a simulated spot lamp's cone, half angles in radians: full intensity inside inner_angle, none beyond outer_angle, with 0 <= inner_angle < outer_angle < pi/2. light_id comes from lighting.get_lighting; the two angles are checked together and applied together, and a refused request leaves the lamp as it was. Answers with current_inner_angle and current_outer_angle, the effective angles after the call.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
          // Half angles below pi/2; the ordering of the two is the engine's
          // to check.
          restrict: {
            inner_angle: { min: 0, max: 1.5707 },
            outer_angle: { min: 0, max: 1.5707 },
          },
        },
        {
          member: "set_light_orientation",
          tool: "lighting.set_light_orientation",
          description: "Set a simulated environment map's orientation, orientation = a unit quaternion [x, y, z, w] rotating the map into the world frame. light_id comes from lighting.get_lighting; only an environment light supports it, and a refused request leaves the light as it was. Answers with current_orientation, the effective quaternion after the call.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
        {
          member: "reset_lighting",
          tool: "lighting.reset_lighting",
          description: "Put every light of the loaded simulated scene back to its authored values. Takes no arguments.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
      ],
    },

    materials: {
      contract: {
        name: "scene_materials",
        tag: "v1",
        // Pinned like `scene` above (`sha256sum simulation/scene_materials.json5`).
        sha256: "ff2ce6127fdc52c97bf3e743297a31ec8bea2d153085baf8ad34e8d1112e2953",
      },

      // Services again, the setters `mutating`; `restrict` narrows the
      // finish's two scalars to the unit interval at the endpoint.
      services: [
        {
          member: "get_materials",
          tool: "materials.get_materials",
          description: "Report every editable material of the loaded simulated scene as a JSON object in materials_json: materials, each with id, label, scope (owner, a robot instance or the scene; surfaces, the count of surfaces sharing it; bodies, their body names) and properties (color_rgb, metallic, roughness), each with unit, default and value, plus min and max for the scalars. Every surface sharing a material changes with it, so the scope is what an edit reaches. success is false, with message saying why, while no scene is loaded. Takes no arguments and changes nothing.",
          operation: "read_only",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
        {
          member: "set_material_color",
          tool: "materials.set_material_color",
          description: "Set a simulated material's base colour on every surface that shares it, color = linear RGB reflectance [r, g, b] in the unit interval. material_id comes from materials.get_materials; a refused request leaves the material as it was. Answers with current_color, the effective colour after the call.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
        {
          member: "set_material_finish",
          tool: "materials.set_material_finish",
          description: "Set a simulated material's finish on every surface that shares it: metallic and roughness, both in the unit interval (0 metallic a dielectric, 1 a conductor; 0 roughness a mirror finish, 1 fully diffuse), checked together and applied together. material_id comes from materials.get_materials; a refused request leaves the material as it was. Answers with current_metallic and current_roughness, the effective values after the call.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
          // The contract's `f64` is unbounded; the metallic-roughness model
          // is defined on the unit interval.
          restrict: {
            metallic: { min: 0, max: 1 },
            roughness: { min: 0, max: 1 },
          },
        },
        {
          member: "reset_materials",
          tool: "materials.reset_materials",
          description: "Put every material of the loaded simulated scene back to its authored values, on every surface that shares each one. Takes no arguments.",
          operation: "mutating",
          deadline_ms: 5000,
        },
      ],
    },
  },
}
